language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ facility_location: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A company is considering opening warehouses in four cities (New York, Los Angeles, Chicago and Atlanta) to meet regional demands at minimal costs. Each open warehouse has a fixed weekly cost and can ship at most max_shipping units per week; a closed one ships nothing. Shipping one unit from a warehouse to a region has a given cost, and each region must receive at least its weekly demand. Decide which warehouses to open so that: if the New York warehouse is opened, then the Los Angeles warehouse must also be opened; no more than three warehouses are operational in any given week; either the Atlanta or the Los Angeles warehouse is opened. The total cost, the fixed costs of the open warehouses plus the shipping costs, is minimised.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `facility_location.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/facility_location.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `facility_location_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py facility_location.json
$   savilerow model.eprime facility_location_1.param -run-solver

$ the four warehouses, in the order New York, Los Angeles, Chicago, Atlanta (indices 1 to 4)
letting cities be 4
$ demands_dim1: the number of regions (derived from the data; not a field of the reader's JSON)
given demands_dim1 : int
letting regions = demands_dim1
$ demands[r]: the weekly demand of region r, indices 1-based (the data's index 0 is demands[1])
given demands : matrix indexed by [int(1..regions)] of int
$ fixed_costs[w]: the fixed weekly cost of warehouse w if it is open, indices 1-based
given fixed_costs : matrix indexed by [int(1..cities)] of int
$ max_shipping: the most units a warehouse can ship per week
given max_shipping : int
$ shipping_costs[w, r]: the cost of shipping one unit from warehouse w to region r, indices 1-based
given shipping_costs : matrix indexed by [int(1..cities), int(1..regions)] of int

$ open_warehouse[w]: warehouse w is open (the output, indices 1-based)
find open_warehouse : matrix indexed by [int(1..cities)] of bool
$ ships[w, r]: the units shipped from warehouse w to region r; zero when w is closed (the output, indices 1-based)
find ships : matrix indexed by [int(1..cities), int(1..regions)] of int(0..max_shipping)
$ total_cost: the fixed costs of the open warehouses plus the shipping costs (the output)
find total_cost : int(0..((sum w : int(1..cities) . fixed_costs[w]) + max_shipping * (sum w : int(1..cities) . sum r : int(1..regions) . shipping_costs[w, r])))

minimising total_cost
such that
$ the total cost is the fixed costs of the open warehouses plus the cost of all shipments
total_cost = (sum w : int(1..cities) . fixed_costs[w] * toInt(open_warehouse[w]))
             + (sum w : int(1..cities) . sum r : int(1..regions) . shipping_costs[w, r] * ships[w, r]),
$ a warehouse ships at most max_shipping units per week, and a closed warehouse ships nothing
forAll w : int(1..cities) . (sum r : int(1..regions) . ships[w, r]) <= max_shipping * toInt(open_warehouse[w]),
$ each region receives at least its weekly demand
forAll r : int(1..regions) . (sum w : int(1..cities) . ships[w, r]) >= demands[r],
$ if the New York warehouse is opened, then the Los Angeles warehouse must also be opened
open_warehouse[1] -> open_warehouse[2],
$ no more than three warehouses are operational
(sum w : int(1..cities) . toInt(open_warehouse[w])) <= 3,
$ either the Atlanta or the Los Angeles warehouse must be opened
open_warehouse[4] \/ open_warehouse[2]
